<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sozh River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sozh is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 650 kilometres long, a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Rising near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, it flows south through eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> past Krychaw and Gomel to join the Dnieper near the Ukrainian border. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its basin was the home of the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radimichians">Radimichs</a>, and it later formed part of the lands of Smolensk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sozh-river.jpg" alt="Sozh River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soz_river.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sozh rises in the Smolensky District of Smolensk Oblast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and is mostly snow-fed. It flows south through the Pochinkovsky and Khislavichsky Districts, then along the border between Khislavichsky and Shumyachsky Districts in Russia and the Mogilev Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> on its left bank. Its length is 648 kilometres, of which 493 lie within Belarus, and its catchment covers 42,140 square kilometres. The river freezes between November and early January, and the ice thaws from late March or April.</p><p>Its main tributaries are the Vikhra and Pronia on the right and the Ostyor, Besed, Iput and Uts on the left. It is one of six tributaries longer than 500 kilometres that join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. The name comes from Old East Slavic Съжь; Vadim Zhuchkevich derived it from an Old Russian word for burned parts of a forest prepared for ploughing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Several centuries ago the East Slavic Radimichi lived in the Sozh basin and founded the town of Gomel. They farmed, reared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, fished and gathered honey, and the river carried their trade towards the north-west and south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Gomel is first reported in 1142 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>. It came under Lithuanian control in 1537, then Polish rule under the Truce of Andrusovo, and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1772. Embankments built along the river there are credited to the Rumyantsevs and the Paskeviches, statesmen and commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p>Vetka, on the river north-east of Gomel, was gutted by Russian forces in 1735 and 1764, and its people were resettled in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It was known for icon painting and wood carving and was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1852. Excavations have found a Palaeolithic boat in the river's soils, now in the Museum of Ancient Belarusian Culture, and the remains of temples dated to the 3rd and 4th centuries at the mounds of Tushemlia and Haradok, which have been inferred to be Baltic. Remains of woolly mammoth have been found along the river as well. Russian myths set the turbulent Sozh against the quiet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Sozh is navigable from Krychaw, with locks in its upper reaches, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> is floated along it. Gomel, the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, stands on it, as do Krychaw, Cherykaw, Slawharad and Vietka. The mouth of the river is 150 metres broad and swampy. Phosphorite lies along its banks between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislavl">Mstislavl</a> and Krichev.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sozh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mstislavl, on the <b>Sozh</b>, was a town of the old Smolensk lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mstislavl">Principality of Mstislavl</a></li><li><blockquote>The Primary Chronicle says the Radimichs descended from Radim, who came with his brother Vyatko from the west and settled on the <b>Sozh</b>, a tributary of the Dnieper in modern Belarus and western Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/radimichs">Radimichs</a></li></ul></section>
